  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 358 AM EDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nOnce the front departs to the south Thursday night, surface high \npressure and dry low-level air will build in over the region, \nallowing for a mostly calm stretch of weather to close the workweek \nand start the weekend. Temperatures will remain below normal, with \nforecasted highs in the mid 80s and lows in the mid to upper 60s \nFriday morning and in the low to mid 60s Saturday morning. A few \nslight chances for rain are possible east of I-75, but most places \nwill remain dry during this stretch. The upper trough over the \neastern CONUS will dig southward on Saturday, with the trough axis \nswinging over our region as well. A shortwave will accompany this \npattern chance, providing another chance for showers and isolated \nstorms to develop. With fairly dry low-level air in place, chances \nfor appreciable rainfall are fairly low (<20%). \n\nOnce the trough axis swings through late Saturday, bringing in \nreinforced dry air and surface high pressure, the rest of the weekend \ninto the start of the following workweek looks to be dry and mild, \ncontinuing the trend for afternoon temperatures in the 80s. The very \nend of the forecast period is a little uncertain, as the sturdy \nridge over the western CONUS becomes less amplified, causing the4 \nupper trough over us to shift to the east. The spread in \ndeterministic and ensemble solutions does present some uncertainty \nin precip chances after Monday, though antecedent low-level dry air \nshould keep probabilities low for the rest of the forecast period \n(<25%).",
